\label{t.lambda}
Let $r\geq 2$ be an integer. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$. One has a sharp dichotomy:
\begin{itemize}
\item either $\Lambda$ is volume-hyperbolic,
\item or  Newhouse phenomena occur $C^r$-close to $\Lambda$
\end{itemize}

\label{t.robustization}
Let $r\geq 2$ be an integer.  Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle containing a saddle $P$  for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$. Assume that $\Lambda$ has no dominated splitting of any index in a set $I\subset {\mathbb N}$. 


Then one can make simultaneously robust  those non-dominations by a $C^r$-perturbation: in any neighborhood ${\mathcal V}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ of $f$, there exists an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {\mathcal V}$ of diffeomorphisms $g$ such that the homoclinic class $\operatorname{Hom}(P_g,g)$ of the continuation $P_g$ of $P$ has no domination of any index $i\in I$.

We point out that these theorems
\begin{itemize}
\item also hold in $C^{1+\alpha}$ topologies; this is discussed in \cref{s.C1alpha}.
\item hold for even more general sets $\Lambda$, namely, for heteroclinic combinations of basic sets under a similar Eulerian condition, which happens to be generic. This is discussed in \cref{s.moregenerallambda}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{align*}{\mathcal C}(P,f)&:=\bigl({\mathcal C}(P,f),P\bigr)\\
\operatorname{Hom}(P,f)&:=\bigl(\operatorname{Hom}(P,f),P\bigr).
\end{align*}

\label{t.comprehensible}
For any integer $r\geq 1$, for any diffeomorphism $f$ in a residual subset ${\mathcal R} \subset {Diff}^r(M)$, 
if the chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P,f)$ of a saddle $P$ has mechanically no domination of any index, that is:

 if, for any index $1\leq i<d$, there is a cycle of basic sets that contains the saddle $P$ and either
\begin{itemize}
\item a periodic point $Q$ of period $p$ such that the eigenvalues $\lambda_1,...,\lambda_d$ of the derivative $Df^p$ at $Q$, counted with multiplicity and ordered by increasing moduli, satisfy $|\lambda_i|=|\lambda_{i+1}|$,
\item or a non-transverse intersection point of the stable and unstable laminations of to two basic sets $K,L$ of index $i$ (possibly $K=L$),
\item or, more generally a bundle tangency of index $i$, as defined later in~section~\ref{s.bundtang},
\end{itemize}
then the homoclinic class $\operatorname{Hom}(P,f)$ is the Hausdorff limit of a sequence of sinks or sources.

\label{t.homoclnodommoregen}
Let $r\geq 1$ be an integer and let $P$ be a saddle point for a diffeomorphism $f$. Let ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ be an open neighborhood of $f$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item the continuation $P_g$ of $P$ is defined for $g\in {\mathcal U}$,
\item for all $1\leq i< d$, there is a dense subset ${\mathcal D}_i \subset {\mathcal U}$ of diffeomorphisms $g$ such that the chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P_g,g)$ has mechanically no domination of index $i$. 
\end{itemize}
Then there is a residual set ${\mathcal R}\subset {\mathcal U}$ of diffeomorphisms $g$ such that the homoclinic class $\operatorname{Hom}(P_g,g)$ is the Hausdorff limit of a sequence of sinks or sources.

\label{t.nodomtang}
 Let $r\geq 1$ be an integer and let $f\in{Diff}^r(M)$. Let $P$ be a saddle orbit for $f$ such that its chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P,f)$ has mechanically no domination of same index as $P$.

 In any $C^r$-neighborhood ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ of $f$ there exists a diffeomorphism $g$ such that $P$ is again a saddle for $g$, with a homoclinic tangency related to it.

