\label{p.hethom}
 For any integer $r\geq 1$, there is a residual subset of $C^r$-diffeomorphisms $f$ such that 
\begin{enumerate}
\item for any saddle point $P$, $\overline{\operatorname{Het}(P,f)}=\operatorname{Hom}(P,f)$.\label{i.1}
\item If two basic sets $K$ and $L$ are heteroclinically related, they are robustly so.
\end{enumerate}

\label{t.robustizationalpha}
Fix an integer $r\geq 1$ and a real number $0<\alpha\leq 1$. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle containing a saddle $P$ for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$. Assume that $\Lambda$ has no dominated splitting of any index in a set $I\subset {\mathbb N}$. Let $C>0$ and let ${\mathcal V}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ be a neighborhood of $f$.

There exists an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^1(M)$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item ${\mathcal U}$ intersects ${\mathcal V}$,
\item if $g\in{\mathcal U}\cap {Diff}^{1+\alpha}_C(M)$, that is, if $g\in {\mathcal U}$ is $C^{1+\alpha}$ with H�lder constant $C$, then the continuated chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P_g,g)$ of $P$ has mechanically no domination of any index $i\in I$.
\end{itemize}

\label{t.lambdaalpha}
Fix real numbers $C>0$ and $0<\alpha<1$. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^{1+\alpha}_C(M)$. One has a sharp dichotomy:
\begin{itemize}
\item either $\Lambda$ is volume-hyperbolic,
\item or there is an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^{1+\alpha}_C(M)$ such that 
 \begin{itemize}
 \item $f$ is in the closure of ${\mathcal U}$
 \item and any $g$ in a residual set ${\mathcal R}\subset {\mathcal U}$ has infinitely many sinks or sources.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\label{t.mechnovolhyp}
For any integer $r\geq 1$, for any diffeomorphism $f$ in a residual subset ${\mathcal R} \subset {Diff}^r(M)$, the following holds:

if a chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P,f)$ is mechanically not volume-hyperbolic, then $\operatorname{Hom}(P,f)$ is the Hausdorff limit of a sequence of sinks or sources.

\begin{align*}
